WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.480
Governance is the system that makes secure cloud behavior repeatable. It is not just a

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.220
policy document and it is not just a security team's dashboard.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.400
Good governance helps teams structure accounts, subscriptions, projects, resources,

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.420
identities, budgets, ownership,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.360
and exceptions so that secure choices are easier to make and risky changes are easier to

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:23.540
find.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.240
Clear structure matters. Accounts, subscriptions, projects, folders,

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.140
and resource groups should reflect how the organization operates.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.600
Production, development, shared services, security tooling,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.020
and experimental work may need different boundaries.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.120
Naming and tagging help people find assets, understand purpose, assign owners,

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.140
track data sensitivity, and route alerts.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.400
A resource with no owner is hard to review. A resource with no environment label is hard

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.900
to prioritize.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.400
A resource with no data classification may be treated too casually. Baselines define

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.780
expected secure configuration.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.040
A baseline might require logging, private storage by default, encryption at rest,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.420
multi-factor authentication for privileged users, restricted administrator roles,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.080
backup policies, tagging, approved regions, or limited public exposure.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.340
The important point is not that every workload is identical. The point is that teams know

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.400
the secure starting point and document exceptions when they need a different design.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:32.020
Infrastructure as code can support governance because it makes configuration reviewable,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.320
repeatable, and versioned.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.420
A change to a network rule, role policy,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.440
or storage setting can be reviewed before deployment.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.620
But infrastructure as code is not automatically secure. Templates must be maintained,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.680
modules must be reviewed, and pipelines must have appropriate permissions.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.860
Otherwise automation can create consistent mistakes at scale. Drift detection helps teams

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.160
find when actual configuration no longer matches the intended baseline.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.180
Drift can come from emergency changes, manual console edits, old experiments,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.120
service updates, or incomplete cleanup.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.820
The response should be practical: identify the owner, understand the reason,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.840
fix the configuration or approve an exception,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.500
and update the baseline if the old rule no longer fits.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.160
Continuous improvement is the right mindset. Cloud security is not finished after the

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.900
first hardening pass.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:32.280
Services change, teams change, applications change, and risk changes. Regular review,

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.940
clear ownership, safe exception handling, posture reporting,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.080
and remediation tracking turn governance into daily operating discipline.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.140
The goal is a cloud environment where teams can move quickly while still knowing what

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.400
exists,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.780
who owns it, which controls apply, and how risky changes are corrected.
